# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/009_shift_semantics.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 009_shift_semantics
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Shift operators with counts strictly in range, including arithmetic right shift of negatives, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every shift count in this program is at least 0 and strictly less than the width of the
promoted left operand: 31 or below for the 32-bit int and unsigned int cases, and 63 or below
for the 64-bit long long and unsigned long long cases, the largest counts used being 62 and
63.  No count is negative and no count equals or exceeds the operand width, so no shift is
undefined -- which matters more here than anywhere else in this area, because an out-of-range
count is the one way a shift program could make a divergence prove nothing about either
compiler.
No negative value is ever shifted left.  No signed left shift produces a value outside the
range of its type: the largest signed left shifts used are 1 << 30 and 1LL << 62, both of
which are representable.  1 << 31 is deliberately never formed; the only left shift that
reaches bit 31 is 1u << 31, whose left operand is unsigned and therefore modular rather than
overflowing.
The unsigned char operand is promoted to int before shifting, so (unsigned char)128 >> 3 is
an int shift and %d is the correct specifier.  A shift count of unsigned char type is
promoted too, and separately: the operands of a shift do not undergo the usual arithmetic
conversions, so 1 << (unsigned char)4 is an int shift governed by the left operand's type
alone.
No signed overflow, no aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, and no object modified
twice between sequence points: every object is initialized in its own declaration and never
assigned to afterwards, so there is no second modification anywhere to sequence against a
first.  No dependence on padding bytes or on the relative addresses of unrelated objects.  No
shift operand is an unparenthesised additive expression -- none is an additive expression at
all -- so the strict warning gate is satisfied with no deviation at all, and the sanitizer
run reports nothing.
NO EVALUATION ORDER IS DEPENDED UPON, and the argument for that deliberately does not rest on
counting the volatile reads, because this program does not hold itself to at most one of them
per call.  The run_shift_zero line passes two value arguments, i_five << sh_zero and
i_five >> sh_zero, so that one call reads four volatile objects and reads each of i_five and
sh_zero twice; five further runtime lines -- run_ushl_max, run_ushr_max, run_sshr_neg2,
run_sshr_all and run_llong_shl -- read two distinct volatile objects as the two operands of a
single shift.  The argument rests on something stronger.  No volatile object is ever WRITTEN:
each is initialized in its own declaration and never assigned to again, so every read of one
yields the value that declaration gave it, and therefore neither the number of accesses --
which C11 6.7.3p7 leaves implementation-defined, so a record may not assume it -- nor the
order they happen in can change a printed byte.  Both permitted orders of the two arguments
produce the same stdout, which is the property the oracle needs, and pairing the two shifts
in one call is also what stops either of them being folded away.  The folded twin of that
line, fold_shift_zero, likewise passes two value arguments, but both are constant expressions
with no side effect at all, so no ordering question arises there.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This program takes NO target restriction, and that is an empirical conclusion rather than an
assumption.  Right shift of a negative signed value is implementation-defined under C11
6.5.7p5 rather than fixed by the standard, so agreement between the four backends has to be
measured and cannot be inferred the way division and remainder can.  It was measured, and it
held: the operation behaves identically on all four supported targets, x86-64, i686, AArch64
and RISC-V 64 -- -8 >> 1 yields -4, -1024 >> 5 yields -32, and -1 >> 31 yields -1 on every
one of them, at every optimization level.  Because the four were observed to agree, any
cross-backend divergence in this program is a genuine finding rather than an
implementation-defined difference, which is precisely why oracle (b) stays enabled here over
the full target list instead of being narrowed away.
The opposite rule is pinned down at the same time, and deliberately: fold_ushr_max and
run_ushr_max shift the unsigned value 2147483648 right by 31 and require the answer 1, which
only a zero-filling shift produces.  A backend that sign-extended an unsigned value, or that
applied one rule to both signednesses, therefore fails this record on one line or the other
rather than passing both halves.
What would legitimately break the conclusion above: a target whose implementation defined the
right shift of a negative value as a logical shift would still be conforming, so the
resulting difference would be an implementation-defined divergence rather than a compiler
defect.  It must in that case be recorded as an expected divergence with this record's target
list narrowed and the reason stated here -- never left to surface as a finding against a
backend that did nothing wrong, and never resolved by dropping the shift operators from the
corpus, which would remove a feature this area is required to cover.
long and unsigned long are deliberately never used, and no object is declared with either.
Their width was measured as 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes on the other three targets, so a
shift of a long would print two different values across backends for reasons that have
nothing to do with the compiler.  Only int and unsigned int, 32 bits on all four targets, and
long long and unsigned long long, 64 bits on all four, appear here.
Plain char is never used as an arithmetic type and no object is declared plain char, because
plain char signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and
RISC-V 64.  The only character data is the format-string literals, whose bytes printf copies
through unchanged; no plain-char value is read as a number, converted, compared or formatted
numerically, so that difference cannot reach the output.  No pointer value is printed and no
header is included, so every bound is spelled literally.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_ushl_max=2147483648
fold_ushr_max=1
fold_sshl_safe=1073741824
fold_sshr_neg1=-4
fold_sshr_neg2=-32
fold_sshr_all=-1
fold_shift_zero=5 5
fold_uchar_promoted=16
fold_count_type=16
fold_llong_shl=4611686018427387904
fold_llong_shr=-4
fold_ullong_shl=9223372036854775808
run_ushl_max=2147483648
run_ushr_max=1
run_sshl_safe=1073741824
run_sshr_neg1=-4
run_sshr_neg2=-32
run_sshr_all=-1
run_shift_zero=5 5
run_uchar_promoted=16
run_count_type=16
run_llong_shl=4611686018427387904
run_llong_shr=-4
run_ullong_shl=9223372036854775808
END
